# apps/web environment. Copy to `.env` (git-ignored) before `pnpm --filter @sam-group/web dev`.
#
# This is the FIRST environment file this application has needed. Everything
# before it rendered from fixtures, and the six Product Family pages still do
# for their editorial content — the variable below is only how they reach
# NestJS for the Category record's canonical identity and name.

# --- API ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The NestJS origin, server-side only.
#
# NOT `NEXT_PUBLIC_`, and that is a decision rather than an omission: apps/api
# boots with `cors: false` because API_CONTRACT_FINAL.md §1 states that no
# browser-originated request ever reaches it. Every read is issued from a Server
# Component. Prefixing this would inline an internal origin into every browser
# bundle and advertise a surface the architecture says does not exist.
#
# ORIGIN ONLY — no path. The `/api/v1` prefix is a frozen architectural constant
# (apps/api `main.ts` sets it globally) and lives in src/lib/api-client.ts, so a
# mistyped .env cannot move the whole API surface.
#
# In development the applications run on the HOST via `pnpm dev`, so this points
# straight at the API's own port. It must match API_PORT in apps/api/.env
# (3001). Going through the local nginx proxy instead would add a hop that can
# fail independently of the thing being served; nginx is the production edge.
#
# In production, web and api are containers behind one nginx (ADR-005) and this
# becomes the compose service name — `http://api:3000`, matching API_UPSTREAM in
# the repository-root .env.
#
# UNSET IS SAFE. A missing or malformed value is reported server-side as an
# unreachable API and every Product Family page renders from its fixture, exactly
# as it did before this variable existed. A fresh clone runs with no .env at all.
#
# BUT SET IT WHEREVER THE LIVE CATEGORY API IS MEANT TO BE READ — at build time as
# well as at runtime. The fail-open path costs nothing at runtime, and it has one
# consequence at build time worth knowing about: with the variable absent, the
# client returns "unreachable" without issuing a request, so nothing marks the six
# proof Category routes as dynamic and `next build` prerenders them from their
# fixtures. Those prerendered pages do not start consulting the API later — that
# happens only in a build or run where the variable is present and the routes are
# actually rendered dynamically.
API_INTERNAL_URL=http://localhost:3001
